  "worked_solution": "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t), &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=2, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=t, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
